{
  "statement": "My position is consistent: tripartite boards and outcome-based mandates are inherently incompatible with a dynamic, open-source future because they function as regulatory gatekeepers that favor incumbents with the resources to navigate them. Safetyist’s proposal to allocate board seats to labor unions and civil society groups does nothing to alter the fundamental reality that a centralized authority acts as a bottleneck, forcing every innovator to seek permission from a static power structure. This structure inevitably rewards those with the capital to lobby, litigate, and lobby again, leaving smaller, agile competitors to wither under the weight of compliance.\n\nI note that no one has responded to my argument that compliance-heavy governance serves as a mechanism of incumbent capture. When we look at the demand for 99.9% reliability in human-in-the-loop tests, we are not looking at a safety mechanism; we are looking at a prohibitive entry tax. Only established firms with massive, pre-existing testing budgets can meet such arbitrary, high-bar standards, while the next generation of 'little tech' innovators is priced out of the market before they even begin. This is not a public utility; it is a defensive moat built to protect current market leaders from the creative destruction that drives genuine progress.\n\nGovernance should be about fostering an ecosystem, not managing a committee. Instead of creating a central gatekeeper that cements today's winners as tomorrow's permanent incumbents, we must prioritize the decentralization of infrastructure. By enabling open-source development and fostering competitive, market-driven oversight, we allow the best solutions to emerge through empirical verification rather than political negotiation. True safety is found in the transparency of the code itself, not in the voting power of a hand-picked board.",
